<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  An office building's elevators: cars that carry people between floors (Pro).

  Five floors above a lobby, 3 cars, a morning peak. People arrive at the lobby (a Poisson stream whose rate ramps up to a peak and back), ride to a random floor, stay
  about 2 minutes, and ride back down. A car is a vehicle on a line: it has a capacity, a speed, a time lost at each stop and a time for each person who gets on or off.
    positions="Lobby:0, F1:3.5, F2:7, ..."    where the stations are on the line (metres); Gate is the lobby again, where people leave (two stations at one coordinate are one stop for a car)
    fleets="Car:3 capacity=8 speed=1.6 stop=4 load=1.2 dispatch=nearest"    3 cars of 8 people, 1.6 m/s, 4 s a stop and 1.2 s per person moved
    carry="Lobby>F1:Car; ... F1>Gate:Car"  the routing edges that a car carries: the person calls a car at the first station and arrives at the second when they get off
  A call goes to the car that can be there soonest and stays with it; a car stops at a floor for people who get off, for calls it is carrying out, and for waiting people
  going its way (their call is then taken over). It keeps its direction while anyone ahead needs it and turns round only at a floor.
  Time is in seconds. Output columns, 9 per row: 0 time, 1 person, 2 station (0 Lobby, 1 to 5 floors, 6 Gate), 3 event type (0 arrive, 1 start, 2 finish, 3 exit, 4 call, 5 board,
  6 alight), 4 queue (people waiting for a car at that station, for a call, board or alight row), 5 busy, 6 people in the building, 7 car (0 to 2, or -1), 8 people in that car.
  The wait for a car is the time from a person's call row to their board row; the ride is from board to alight.

  To run it:        grind with a count of 20000 on samples/pro/process-flow-elevators.xml  (the run ends at 3,600 s)
  What to look for: averaged over 10 seeds (the whole hour), the mean wait for a car is about 7 s at the lobby and 9.5 s on the floors with 3 cars; with the declare cars set
                    to 2 it is 95 s at the lobby and 35 s on the floors, and with 4 cars 3.3 s and 5.3 s. The rule declare set to least-loaded is slightly worse here (8 s and 11 s).
-->
<dataset columns="time, person, station, type, queue, busy, inSystem, car, inCar" seed="20261015">
  <definitions>
    <declare name="cars" value="3"/>
    <declare name="rule" value="nearest"/>
  </definitions>
  <root node="Building"/>
  <nodes>
    <Exponential name="Arrival" lambda="1"/>
    <Constant name="Instant" value="0"/>
    <Constant name="InstantGate" value="0"/>
    <Exponential name="Stay" lambda="0.0083333"/>
    <ProcessFlow name="Building" stations="Lobby, F[5]:inf, Gate" entry="Lobby" until="3600"
                 routing="Lobby>F1:0.2; Lobby>F2:0.2; Lobby>F3:0.2; Lobby>F4:0.2; Lobby>F5:0.2; F1>Gate:1; F2>Gate:1; F3>Gate:1; F4>Gate:1; F5>Gate:1"
                 rate="0:0.02, 900:0.2, 2700:0.2, 3600:0.02" rateMode="linear"
                 positions="Lobby:0, F1:3.5, F2:7, F3:10.5, F4:14, F5:17.5, Gate:0"
                 fleets="Car:${cars} capacity=8 speed=1.6 stop=4 load=1.2 dispatch=${rule}"
                 carry="Lobby>F1:Car; Lobby>F2:Car; Lobby>F3:Car; Lobby>F4:Car; Lobby>F5:Car; F1>Gate:Car; F2>Gate:Car; F3>Gate:Car; F4>Gate:Car; F5>Gate:Car">
      <input name="Arrival" role="arrival"/>
      <input name="Instant" role="service.Lobby"/>
      <input name="Stay" role="service.F"/>
      <input name="InstantGate" role="service.Gate"/>
    </ProcessFlow>
  </nodes>
</dataset>
